1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,360
Burning an alcohol completely breaks it right down,

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:05,680
to carbon dioxide and water.

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:08,800
Today, three reactions that turn an alcohol into

4
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:10,800
a new compound instead.

5
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:11,920
Here's a clue.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,120
This orange solution is an oxidising agent.

7
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,760
Warm it with ethanol, and it turns green.

8
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,520
Warm it with two-methylpropan-two-ol, another alcohol, and it

9
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:22,800
stays orange.

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:25,600
So where can an alcohol reaction lead, and

11
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:26,560
what decides it?

12
00:00:27,767 --> 00:00:31,926
First, dehydration, which means removing water.

13
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:34,566
The O H leaves from one carbon, and

14
00:00:34,647 --> 00:00:37,526
a hydrogen leaves from the carbon next door.

15
00:00:37,847 --> 00:00:40,086
Together, they make a water molecule.

16
00:00:40,167 --> 00:00:43,126
The two carbons, each now one bond short,

17
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:44,966
form a double bond.

18
00:00:45,047 --> 00:00:48,326
So ethanol gives ethene, and water.

19
00:00:48,367 --> 00:00:51,766
The conditions go on the arrow: concentrated sulfuric

20
00:00:51,847 --> 00:00:55,366
acid, or concentrated phosphoric acid, at about one

21
00:00:55,447 --> 00:00:56,966
hundred and seventy degrees.

22
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:00,166
If that looks familiar, it should.

23
00:01:00,567 --> 00:01:02,646
It's hydration, run backwards.

24
00:01:02,887 --> 00:01:05,686
To add water to ethene, we used steam,

25
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:06,806
at high pressure.

26
00:01:07,047 --> 00:01:10,166
To take water out, we use concentrated acid,

27
00:01:10,287 --> 00:01:12,966
which soaks up water, and the alkene escapes

28
00:01:13,047 --> 00:01:14,126
from the hot mixture.

29
00:01:14,327 --> 00:01:15,846
It's the same equilibrium.

30
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:19,206
From Module five, adding water pushes it towards

31
00:01:19,287 --> 00:01:22,246
the alcohol, and removing water pushes it towards

32
00:01:22,327 --> 00:01:23,286
the alkene.

33
00:01:23,367 --> 00:01:26,086
So never write water as a reactant, or

34
00:01:26,247 --> 00:01:28,406
dilute acid, for dehydration.

35
00:01:29,634 --> 00:01:31,553
With a longer alcohol, there can be a

36
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:32,193
choice.

37
00:01:32,674 --> 00:01:35,553
In butan-two-ol, the O H carbon has a

38
00:01:35,634 --> 00:01:37,073
neighbour on each side.

39
00:01:37,474 --> 00:01:39,473
The hydrogen can leave from either one.

40
00:01:39,954 --> 00:01:43,153
So you can get but-one-ene, or but-two-ene.

41
00:01:43,634 --> 00:01:46,353
Both form, and but-two-ene is usually the main

42
00:01:46,434 --> 00:01:46,993
product.

43
00:01:47,554 --> 00:01:50,433
If a question asks for every possible product,

44
00:01:50,554 --> 00:01:51,233
give both.

45
00:01:52,434 --> 00:01:56,873
Second, substitution with a hydrogen halide, H X.

46
00:01:57,154 --> 00:01:59,593
The halogen takes the O H's place.

47
00:01:59,874 --> 00:02:02,993
Ethanol and hydrogen bromide give bromoethane.

48
00:02:03,314 --> 00:02:04,993
And the O H doesn't vanish.

49
00:02:05,314 --> 00:02:07,153
It joins the H from H B r,

50
00:02:07,314 --> 00:02:08,193
to make water.

51
00:02:08,834 --> 00:02:11,473
We heat it under reflux, so volatile chemicals

52
00:02:11,554 --> 00:02:13,393
drip back instead of escaping.

53
00:02:13,794 --> 00:02:16,113
It's the ethanol video's route, run the other

54
00:02:16,194 --> 00:02:16,833
way.

55
00:02:17,154 --> 00:02:19,713
There, hydroxide pushed out a halogen.

56
00:02:20,114 --> 00:02:22,753
Here, a halogen pushes out the O H.

57
00:02:23,967 --> 00:02:28,366
Careful, because hydrogen bromide also adds to alkenes.

58
00:02:28,567 --> 00:02:30,686
So how do you tell the two apart?

59
00:02:30,767 --> 00:02:33,486
Look at the starting molecule, and the products.

60
00:02:33,567 --> 00:02:35,966
An alkene and H B r join into

61
00:02:36,087 --> 00:02:38,446
one molecule, with nothing left over.

62
00:02:38,567 --> 00:02:39,806
That's addition.

63
00:02:39,887 --> 00:02:42,126
An alcohol plus H B r gives a

64
00:02:42,247 --> 00:02:44,126
haloalkane and water.

65
00:02:44,207 --> 00:02:45,646
That's substitution.

66
00:02:46,867 --> 00:02:48,546
Third, oxidation.

67
00:02:49,107 --> 00:02:53,106
Here, an oxidising agent removes two hydrogens: the

68
00:02:53,187 --> 00:02:55,426
H from the O H, and an H

69
00:02:55,507 --> 00:02:56,706
from the carbon holding it.

70
00:02:57,347 --> 00:02:59,586
That carbon then forms a double bond to

71
00:02:59,627 --> 00:03:00,306
the oxygen.

72
00:03:00,947 --> 00:03:03,186
A C double bond O is called a

73
00:03:03,267 --> 00:03:04,226
carbonyl group.

74
00:03:04,867 --> 00:03:07,106
We write the oxidant as O in square

75
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:10,146
brackets, and the hydrogens it removes leave as

76
00:03:10,227 --> 00:03:11,106
water.

77
00:03:11,587 --> 00:03:15,266
A common oxidant is acidified potassium dichromate, the

78
00:03:15,347 --> 00:03:16,866
orange solution from the start.

79
00:03:17,347 --> 00:03:19,346
It turns from orange to green as it

80
00:03:19,587 --> 00:03:19,986
reacts.

81
00:03:20,627 --> 00:03:23,826
Dichromate is toxic and can cause cancer, so

82
00:03:23,907 --> 00:03:26,786
schools use small amounts, with gloves and goggles,

83
00:03:26,827 --> 00:03:27,986
and collect the waste.

84
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,320
Now the clue makes sense, because oxidation needs

85
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,880
a hydrogen on the O H carbon.

86
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:38,800
A secondary alcohol, like propan-two-ol, has one.

87
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,120
Remove it, and the carbonyl forms in the

88
00:03:41,240 --> 00:03:44,320
middle of the chain: propanone, a ketone.

89
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:49,360
A tertiary alcohol, like two-methylpropan-two-ol, has none.

90
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:52,080
Its carbon is already bonded to three carbons

91
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:53,440
and the oxygen.

92
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:56,560
Ordinary oxidants can't remove a hydrogen that isn't

93
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,680
there, so the dichromate stays orange.

94
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,500
A primary alcohol, like ethanol, has two hydrogens

95
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:05,780
on that carbon.

96
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:08,260
Oxidise it once, and you get a carbonyl

97
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:10,660
at the end of the chain: ethanal, an

98
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:11,660
aldehyde.

99
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:14,820
But an aldehyde's carbonyl carbon still holds a

100
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:15,860
hydrogen.

101
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:18,660
So it can be oxidised again, to ethanoic

102
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:21,220
acid, a carboxylic acid.

103
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:24,580
A ketone's carbonyl carbon has no hydrogen left,

104
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:26,500
so it stops at the ketone.

105
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,700
So how do you stop at the aldehyde?

106
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:31,420
Use boiling points.

107
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,580
Ethanal has no O H, so its molecules

108
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:36,820
can't hydrogen bond to each other.

109
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,540
It boils at about twenty degrees, far below

110
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:40,740
ethanol.

111
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:44,020
Heat gently and distil: the aldehyde boils off

112
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:46,980
as it forms, before it meets more oxidant.

113
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:49,620
For the acid, heat under reflux with excess

114
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:50,740
oxidant.

115
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:52,980
The aldehyde drips back into the flask, and

116
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:55,060
keeps reacting until it's an acid.

117
00:04:56,267 --> 00:04:57,866
One warning about the colour.

118
00:04:58,427 --> 00:05:01,226
Green tells you something was oxidised, so the

119
00:05:01,307 --> 00:05:04,746
alcohol was primary or secondary, not tertiary.

120
00:05:05,307 --> 00:05:07,466
It doesn't tell you which class, or which

121
00:05:07,547 --> 00:05:08,906
product formed.

122
00:05:08,987 --> 00:05:11,426
For that, you need the conditions, and a

123
00:05:11,467 --> 00:05:12,666
test on the product.

124
00:05:13,867 --> 00:05:16,506
Put it together, and an alcohol is a

125
00:05:16,587 --> 00:05:16,986
hub.

126
00:05:17,467 --> 00:05:19,466
You can make it from an alkene, or

127
00:05:19,627 --> 00:05:20,746
a haloalkane.

128
00:05:21,227 --> 00:05:22,986
And it can lead to an alkene, a

129
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:27,546
haloalkane, or, by oxidation, an aldehyde, ketone or

130
00:05:27,627 --> 00:05:28,106
acid.

131
00:05:28,587 --> 00:05:30,746
Each arrow has its own conditions.

132
00:05:31,967 --> 00:05:35,046
So, dehydration takes out water, and makes an

133
00:05:35,087 --> 00:05:36,206
alkene.

134
00:05:36,327 --> 00:05:39,086
Substitution swaps the O H for a halogen,

135
00:05:39,167 --> 00:05:40,766
and makes water too.

136
00:05:40,847 --> 00:05:43,406
For oxidation, count the hydrogens on the O

137
00:05:43,567 --> 00:05:44,686
H carbon.

138
00:05:44,927 --> 00:05:47,566
Primary goes all the way, secondary stops at

139
00:05:47,647 --> 00:05:50,126
ketone, tertiary won't budge.

140
00:05:50,207 --> 00:05:52,766
And to choose the primary product: distil to

141
00:05:52,887 --> 00:05:55,486
catch the aldehyde, reflux to go all the

142
00:05:55,607 --> 00:05:55,966
way.

143
00:05:57,167 --> 00:05:57,806
Your turn.

144
00:05:58,367 --> 00:06:01,726
Propan-one-ol is used in two separate experiments.

145
00:06:01,967 --> 00:06:05,486
In experiment one, it's heated with concentrated sulfuric

146
00:06:05,647 --> 00:06:06,046
acid.

147
00:06:06,527 --> 00:06:09,566
In experiment two, it's heated under reflux with

148
00:06:09,727 --> 00:06:10,766
hydrogen bromide.

149
00:06:11,407 --> 00:06:14,366
For each experiment, name the organic product, and

150
00:06:14,447 --> 00:06:16,486
identify the type of reaction.

151
00:06:16,767 --> 00:06:18,126
Pause here to answer.

152
00:06:28,534 --> 00:06:32,853
With concentrated acid and heat, propan-one-ol loses water.

153
00:06:33,174 --> 00:06:35,653
That's dehydration, giving propene.

154
00:06:36,134 --> 00:06:39,853
With hydrogen bromide, bromine replaces the O H.

155
00:06:40,054 --> 00:06:44,373
That's substitution, giving one-bromopropane, and water.

156
00:06:44,694 --> 00:06:47,573
Calling the second one addition is the slip.

157
00:06:47,814 --> 00:06:51,093
Addition needs a double bond, and propan-one-ol hasn't

158
00:06:51,174 --> 00:06:51,813
got one.

159
00:06:53,034 --> 00:06:53,913
Last one.

160
00:06:54,154 --> 00:06:58,793
Butan-two-ol and two-methylbutan-two-ol are each heated under

161
00:06:58,874 --> 00:07:02,873
reflux with excess acidified potassium dichromate solution.

162
00:07:03,514 --> 00:07:06,553
For each alcohol, predict the colour change, and

163
00:07:06,634 --> 00:07:08,393
the organic product, if any.

164
00:07:08,874 --> 00:07:09,993
Explain the difference.

165
00:07:10,554 --> 00:07:11,833
Pause here to answer.

166
00:07:22,234 --> 00:07:24,073
Butan-two-ol is secondary.

167
00:07:24,554 --> 00:07:27,193
Its O H carbon has one hydrogen, so

168
00:07:27,354 --> 00:07:30,673
it's oxidised to butan-two-one, a ketone, and the

169
00:07:30,714 --> 00:07:32,393
solution turns green.

170
00:07:32,714 --> 00:07:35,113
Two-methylbutan-two-ol is tertiary.

171
00:07:35,514 --> 00:07:37,833
There's no hydrogen on that carbon, so there's

172
00:07:37,914 --> 00:07:40,033
no reaction, and it stays orange.

173
00:07:40,314 --> 00:07:43,113
Reflux doesn't turn a ketone into an acid,

174
00:07:43,234 --> 00:07:45,593
so butanoic acid is the trap.

175
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,080
So the alcohol's class, and the conditions, decide

176
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:51,040
where it leads.

177
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:55,040
Next: aldehydes and ketones up close.

178
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:57,200
How we name them, and how a silver

179
00:07:57,280 --> 00:07:58,720
mirror tells them apart.

180
00:07:59,934 --> 00:08:01,373
Here are the key notes.

181
00:08:01,614 --> 00:08:03,533
Pause the video if you need more time

182
00:08:03,614 --> 00:08:04,413
to copy them.
